{"id":"4f453ef4-fb55-4d2f-9ca7-99d79ee9144e","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885630,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    nodes_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children_map.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    retained = set(nodes_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_map.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        missing_symbol = False\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            count = sum(1 for nid2 in retained if nodes_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if node['symbol'] == sym and count == 1:\\n                missing_symbol = True\\n                break\\n        if missing_symbol:\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) based on the problem description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" from \"SE-PE-01\". Input payload: nodes (list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), required_symbols. Output: removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe algorithm: Given a tree, we need to remove nodes that are removable while preserving required_symbols. The approach: deeper nodes first, then shallower (depth descending). For each node considered, if removable and it's not required (by symbol), and after removal all required symbols still exist somewhere in the tree (i.e., not only that node has that symbol), then we can remove it. Also, if node has children that are retained? Actually typical parse tree reduction: if we remove a node, its children become children of its parent (i.e., we replace the node with its children). But here it says \"removable\" field indicates if node can be removed? And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Possibly we need to consider that removal means deleting node and its entire subtree? But the candidate description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". I suspect this is reduction similar to Perses: remove subtrees that are not needed for grammar validity. Given the simple payloads, probably we need to remove removable nodes that are not required, in order of decreasing depth, and if removal would cause loss of a required symbol, skip.\n\nLet's parse the candidate_artifact text: \"Implement `grammar_guided_node_reduction` through `solve(case)` ... Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" but later: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That last part suggests we must check that a node's children are not retained (i.e., not in the final set of nodes after removal of others). So we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Means after removal, required symbols still present somewhere.\n\nThe algorithm likely: Sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID. For each node, check if removable and not root and its symbol not required, and after removing it, the required symbols still exist in the tree (consider that maybe other nodes have that symbol). Additionally, node must have no retained children: but since we process in depth order, children are deeper, so they would be processed before parent. So if we removed a child, then the parent no longer has that child, so parent may become removable if it has no children left. So we need to track which nodes are removed. Build a set of removed IDs. While processing in depth descending, for each node that is removable and not removed and not necessary, check if all required symbols are present in nodes that are not removed and not itself (since we temporarily remove it). Also check if node has any children that are not removed. If no children (i.e., none of its children IDs are in retained_nodes set), then we can remove it. Add to removed_nodes set. At the end, removed_node_ids list in order of removal, retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nBut careful: \"retained_node_ids\" must be sorted. The order of removed_node_ids is the actual removal order. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nWe also have \"registered_candidate_order\" which might refer to order we consider candidates. So we iterate in candidate order: descending depth, then by node ID as tiebreak (since stable tie at equal depth). That matches \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nThus the algorithm:\n\n1. Build a dict of node by id.\n2. Build a dict of parent->children list.\n3. Determine root: node with parent=None.\n4. Build initial set of all node ids.\n5. required_symbols list.\n6. Create sets: removed = set(), retained = set(all_ids). Initially all retained.\n7. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (string). Use the list of nodes from payload.\n8. For each