removed because they had retained children themselves? That would block removal of children, so parent removal might be blocked because children are retained. But if later some deeper grandchildren removed, children become removable, but our loop has already passed the children. So we might miss that scenario. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" This implies we process in depth order, but it doesn't re-evaluate nodes after removal of their descendants. In Perses, removal of a node replaces it with its children, so the node itself disappears, and its children become children of the parent. So the node is removed, not the children. But here the \"removable\" flag per node and the rule \"Never remove... a node with retained children\" suggests we only remove a node if it has no children that are retained. That means if it has children, we cannot remove it. So we would only remove leaf nodes initially, then parents become leaf after children removed. To capture that, we need to either iterate multiple times or process in a way that after removing a child, we revisit its parent. The simplest: sort by depth descending, and for each node, if it's removable and conditions met, remove it. Then, because we process deeper first, when we remove a leaf, its parent may become leaf after all children removed. But our loop will eventually reach the parent (since parent is shallower). At that point, if all children were removed, parent's children list will be empty, so it can be removed. That works if we process all nodes in one pass in depth descending order and dynamically check retained children. However, what if a node has multiple children; after processing first child and removing it, the parent still has other children, so we don't remove parent until later if all children get removed. Our loop will process the parent after all children (since children deeper). If some children were not removed (e.g., because they are required symbols), then parent cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nBut consider: child is removable but has its own child that's not removable. Then child cannot be removed because it has retained children. So parent cannot be removed because child is retained. That's fine.\n\nThus one pass in depth descending order with dynamic check should work because children are always deeper and processed before parent. So when we assess parent, the retained set reflects all removals that happened (children, grandchildren etc).\n\nAlso need to check required symbols: for each required symbol, check if any node with that symbol is not removed (excluding current node). If only current node has that symbol, cannot remove.\n\nImportant: The root is not removable. Also, nodes with required symbols as per required_symbols list are not removable. The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we skip if symbol in required_symbols even if removable flag is True.\n\nNow implement solve(case). case is the payload object. It has 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. Also '_case_nonce' but not needed.\n\nWe need to return dict with removed_node_ids (list in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list).\n\nLet's write code.\n\nWe'll map node id to node dict, and build parent->children mapping. We'll also need to get root.\n\nSort nodes: need list of node dicts sorted by depth descending, then id.\n\nPotential issue: depth might not be present? It is present in fixture. Use node['depth'].\n\nTiebreak: use node['id'] as string.\n\nNow check removal conditions:\n\ndef can_remove(node):\n    if node['id'] == root_id: return False\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set: return False\n    if not node['removable']: return False\n    # check children retained\n    for child_id in children_map.get(node['id'], []):\n        if child_id in retained: # child not removed\n            return False\n    # check required symbols still present\n    for sym