retained (since n4 not removed). So has retained child, cannot remove. n3: removable false, skip. Then depth0 root: skip. So removed: n6. retained: all others: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root (root last alphabetically? id root vs n1: root < n1? Actually string comparison: 'n1' > 'root' because 'n' > 'r'? ASCII: 'n' = 110, 'r' = 114, so 'n1' < 'root'? Let's check: 'n1': 'n','1'; 'root': 'r','o','o','t'. 'n' < 'r', so 'n1' < 'root'. So sorted order: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut wait, n4 has symbol s1 which is required, but n4 is not removed. However, n4 is depth2, retained. That's fine.\n\nNow check second fixture: similar but different utilities, but same logic.\n\nThus code works.\n\nNow need to write solve(case) function.\n\nWe must output JSON with \"implementation\" key containing Python source code as a string. So we need to produce that JSON.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nLet's draft the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    nodes_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n.get('parent')\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children_map.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_set = set()\n    retained = set(nodes_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        # Conditions\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_map.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols still present after removal\n        # We will simulate removal: consider retained set without this node\n        # For each required symbol, check if there is at least one node with that symbol in retained minus this node\n        missing_symbol = False\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            count = sum(1 for nid2 in retained if nodes_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym)\n            # if this node's symbol is sym and count == 1, it would be lost\n            if node['symbol'] == sym and count == 1:\n                missing_symbol = True\n                break\n        if missing_symbol:\n            continue\n        # Remove node\n        removed_set.add(nid)\n        retained.discard(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nBut careful: `children_map` might not have entries for leaf nodes. Use `get(nid, [])`.\n\nAlso need to handle case where root_id not found? Shouldn't happen.\n\nNow generate JSON with escaping.\n\nWe'll produce a JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"} with proper escaping.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nWe'll write the output.\n\nAlso note: the output must be exactly that JSON, no extra spaces? The contract says \"first non-whitespace character must be { and the last non-whitespace character must be }\". So we can minify or pretty, but must be valid JSON.\n\nI'll produce minified JSON for reliability.\n\nLet's construct the string.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    nodes_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        